WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.410
Hela in New York, a badass Loki twirling knives,
Valkyrie scenes, and alternate end-credits

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.220
are just a few of the things cut from the
theatrical release of Thor: Ragnarok.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.410
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm discussing Thor: Ragnarok deleted

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:18.900
and alternate scenes, reshoots, rejected concepts,
and everything else we never got to see in

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:19.980
the third Thor movie!

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.619
While you're here, my Thor: Ragnarok giveaway
is still running, so for your chance to win

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:29.759
a Ragnarok Funko Pop or a very cool Obsessed
With Marvel book, just make sure you're subscribed

00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:33.940
to my channel and leave a comment below with
your thoughts about the movie or the deleted

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:34.940
scenes.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:39.109
Obviously, I'll be talking spoilers, so take
care if you haven't seen the film yet.

00:00:39.109 --> 00:00:42.899
OK, let's start with the deleted scenes from
the trailers that never made it in to the

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:43.899
final movie.

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:47.500
The most significant change is the scene of
Hela destroying Thor's hammer, the setting

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.129
for which was moved from New York to Norway.

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:54.420
Reshooting this scene was much more important
than it might at first appear because it completely

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.649
changed how the movie dealt with Odin and
his death.

00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:02.320
During the film's production, set photos popped
up of Anthony Hopkins dressed as a hobo.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.250
At the time, it was reported that in the film
we'd discover that, after Loki had usurped

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.230
Odin from his throne on Asgard, Odin had escaped
to Earth where he was wandering the streets

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.120
of New York penniless, making dire predictions
of the end of the world.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:19.210
In the final movie though, we discover Odin
in Norway instead, contemplating life in a

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.480
field overlooking the sea.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.110
Director Taika Waititi has explained that
he moved the location to Norway because it

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:29.220
felt like a more appropriate setting for Odin
to have a heart-to-heart talk with his sons

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.990
before he died.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.590
Waititi also implied that perhaps feedback
from test screenings was that the audience

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:39.090
didn't understand why a character as powerful
as Odin was now a homeless person bumbling

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.120
around New York.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:45.070
So, the director felt that an open green field
in Norway was more respectful of the mighty

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.230
king Odin.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:50.560
The location also ties in with real-world
Norse legends which are, of course, the basis

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.770
for the Thor Marvelverse.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:56.750
I also liked the way that when Odin died,
his body evaporated into flecks of light mirroring

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:01.290
what happened to his wife Frigga during her
ceremonial funeral in Thor: The Dark World.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:05.370
I can see that this is a more noble death
for the former king of Asgard than perishing

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.350
on the streets of New York.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.200
The alleyway scene in New York was pretty
cool in the trailer, though, and did a great

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.600
job of hyping fans for the movie, even if
the reshot scene felt more appropriate in

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.280
the context of the film.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.690
And that's not the only great moment in the
trailer that ended up being chopped from the

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:22.690
movie.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:26.670
There's several moments cut from the final
showdown on the Rainbow Bridge, including

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.390
the four Revengers squaring off against Hela.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.600
Sadly, in the final film, we never saw our
four heroes line up like this.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.950
Instead, there was a moment of Thor, Valkyrie
and Loki together, but Hulk was busy battling

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.470
Fenris Wolf at the time.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:45.540
It also looks like some extended fight scenes
with Hela might have been chopped as I don't

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.030
think this shot of Hela spinning and sending
out a swirl of knives made it in.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:55.090
And this iconic shot of Hela with her full
headgear turning slowly to the camera appears

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.800
to have been cut from Valkyrie's flashback
scene.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:03.440
Now, there's a very intriguing deleted scene
with Loki twirling a pair of knives on a bridge.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.380
It feels like this might have been part of
a longer deleted scene where Loki helped the

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.069
Asgardian refugees down from their mountain
shelter to the Rainbow Bridge where the rescue

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:13.370
ship was waiting.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:17.621
Loki did get some good action scenes in the
final battle, but it still feels a shame that

00:03:17.621 --> 00:03:18.910
we never saw this moment.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.970
I guess it was cut for time to prevent the
final showdown from getting too long.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:28.100
Speaking of Loki, in an early story draft
there was going to be more of the God of Mischief

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:32.480
via some flashback scenes to a young Loki
antagonising his brother Thor.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.840
The tone Taika Waititi wanted for those flashbacks
was similar to the classic 80s films of writer-director

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.550
John Hughes, such as Sixteen Candles.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:44.820
One of the flashbacks would have popped up
during the scene on Sakaar where Loki is tied

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:49.140
up and Valkyrie, Banner and Thor are all talking
about how he wants to kill them, which leads

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.890
Thor to recount the time when he and Loki
were eight years old, and Loki transformed

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:57.190
himself into a snake, before quickly transforming
back and stabbing Thor.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.070
A flashback here could have been a fun moment,
but according to Waititi, in the end, it was

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:05.120
one thing too many to add to the movie and
the story was funnier the way Chris Hemsworth

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.990
told it.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:11.290
Waititi also revealed to MTV that another
John Hughes-inspired scene ended up cut from

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:12.290
the movie.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.610
The idea was that when Thor and Valkyrie met
in the film they realised that they already

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:16.610
knew each other.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:20.499
Then there was going to be a flashback to
1980s Asgard where everyone had mullets and

00:04:20.499 --> 00:04:22.049
wore huge shoulder pads.

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:25.629
In the cut scene, Thor was going to be an
overweight child who was being picked on by

00:04:25.629 --> 00:04:30.570
other kids and Loki would have been an "emo
goth" character a bit like Draco Malfoy from

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:31.639
Harry Potter.

00:04:31.639 --> 00:04:35.090
The problem with this scene was that, in the
final story, they needed Thor not to have

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:38.509
met Valkyrie before, so the flashback wouldn't
have worked.

00:04:38.509 --> 00:04:44.219
There's also a deleted promo scene between
Dr Strange and Thor at Strange's Sanctum Santorum.

00:04:44.219 --> 00:04:50.400
"Destiny has dire plans for you my friend."

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:51.400
"Well I have dire plans for Destiny!"

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.879
I wonder if this deleted scene suggests that
Doctor Strange had some foreknowledge of the

00:04:54.879 --> 00:04:57.400
events of Ragnarok that are about to be unleashed?

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:01.849
There's also a nice Star Wars-esque shot and
line that was teased in the trailer but ended

00:05:01.849 --> 00:05:05.159
up cut from the movie as well.

00:05:05.159 --> 00:05:06.900
"Hi there!"

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.480
In an interview with Rolling Stone, Tessa
Thompson, who plays Valkyrie, revealed that

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.479
a scene alluding to her character's bisexuality
was cut from the final film.

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:18.740
Thompson said that after researching her character
in the comics, she learned about Valkyrie's

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:23.349
relationship with archaeologist Annabelle
Riggs, so she convinced Waititi to film a

00:05:23.349 --> 00:05:26.759
scene of a woman leaving her bedroom, implying
the two had been intimate.

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:30.970
However, according to the interview, the moment
was deleted because it took focus away from

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.539
the main narrative at the time.

00:05:32.539 --> 00:05:36.900
But with Valkyrie back for a small role in
Avengers: Infinity War, and Thompson having

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:41.850
recently pitched an all-female team-up movie
to Marvel, Valkyrie's sexuality could be something

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.680
that's addressed in a future movie.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.010
And talking of female Asgardian warriors,
one fan favourite missing from the movie is

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:50.010
Lady Sif.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:54.780
Jaimie Alexander recently confirmed that she
was indeed asked to return to the role, but

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.650
scheduling conflicts with her TV series Blindspot
meant she couldn't make it.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:02.979
The curious thing though is that back in 2015,
Jaimie Alexander said she would definitely

00:06:02.979 --> 00:06:06.870
be back for the latest Thor film and that
she would have a pivotal role.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:10.669
This makes me wonder whether, at that time,
Valkyrie wasn't supposed to feature in the

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:14.740
movie, or at least not as heavily, and whether
the original plan was for Sif to play a part

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.139
similar to that instead.

00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:20.900
I guess Alexander's lead in Blindspot, the
first two seasons of which are over 20 episodes,

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:24.310
meant it was too hard to give Sif a major
role in the new film.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:28.870
But at least her absence means Sif wasn't
killed off by Hela as happened to the Warriors

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:29.870
Three.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:34.599
So, Sif could still reappear in the MCU, which
Alexander says she'd be very happy to do.

00:06:34.599 --> 00:06:38.719
Beta Ray Bill is a popular character from
the comics and although there's an easter

00:06:38.719 --> 00:06:44.639
egg to him via a statue outside the Grandmaster's
palace, he never actually appears in the movie.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:48.539
According to Kevin Feige, though, at one point
Beta Ray Bill was going to be in the film,

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:52.150
however, they felt they weren't doing his
character justice so they canned his appearance

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:57.069
altogether and just left the easter egg, though
they may well introduce him in a later movie.

00:06:57.069 --> 00:07:02.189
Hela was one of the highlights of Thor: Ragnarok
thanks to an interesting backstory, great

00:07:02.189 --> 00:07:04.059
swagger, and an actress to match.

00:07:04.059 --> 00:07:09.110
She's also the first female lead villain in
an MCU movie, however, Taika Waititi revealed

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:13.569
that another Marvel comics villain called
Perrikus was originally going to be in the

00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:14.569
film.

00:07:14.569 --> 00:07:17.940
Waititi didn't elaborate on whether the character
would have been instead of Hela, however,

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.740
it's interesting that Perrikus, who's a much
more obscure villain, shares some similar

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:24.830
comic book history to Hela's arc in Thor:
Ragnarok.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:28.729
In the comics, Perrikus is a Dark God, who
is defeated by Odin when he and the other

00:07:28.729 --> 00:07:30.449
Dark Gods attack Asgard.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:34.930
To punish Perrikus, Odin imprisons him in
a special cell in a distant part of the universe

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:39.129
that only the Allfather has access to, in
a similar way to how he keeps Hela locked

00:07:39.129 --> 00:07:41.039
away in the MCU.

00:07:41.039 --> 00:07:45.349
Also like Hela, Perrikus destroyed Thor's
hammer when he chopped it in half in the comics.

00:07:45.349 --> 00:07:49.179
Some of the parallels between the two characters
are close enough that I wonder how much Perrikus

00:07:49.179 --> 00:07:52.270
provided inspiration for Hela's backstory
in the movie.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:56.449
I'm still glad that Hela was the final choice
though as she gave us one of the better MCU

00:07:56.449 --> 00:08:00.770
villains so far, and I'm also glad she didn't
end up being the villain in Thor: The Dark

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.210
World, as was originally going to be the case.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.259
Another Ragnarok highlight is Jeff Goldblum's
take on the Grandmaster, with the veteran

00:08:08.259 --> 00:08:12.180
actor bringing some great off-the-wall humour
to his eccentric Sakaaran dictator.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:16.590
However, if you look at the comic-book version
of his character, you'll notice he has blue

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:17.590
skin.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.419
According to Taika Waititi, the reason for
the change in the movie was that Goldblum

00:08:20.419 --> 00:08:25.289
already played the part of a blue alien in
the 1988 sci-fi musical comedy, Earth Girls

00:08:25.289 --> 00:08:26.289
Are Easy.

00:08:26.289 --> 00:08:31.119
Still, Ragnarok does include some little allusions
to the Grandmaster's original comic-book look,

00:08:31.119 --> 00:08:35.039
with blue eyeliner and a little blue strip
on his face, which is also a nod to the fact

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:37.709
he's related to the MCU's Collector.

00:08:37.709 --> 00:08:42.460
Set visits for Thor: Ragnarok also reported
seeing concept art of a meeting between the

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:45.459
Grandmaster and Guardians of the Galaxy character
The Broker.

00:08:45.459 --> 00:08:49.610
In the first Guardians movie, The Broker planned
to buy the Orb which contained the Power Stone

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:50.800
for the Collector.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:55.050
SlashFilm also reported that an item called
a Xandarian Cube was included on the prop

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:58.860
list in the movie's production office and
that concept art of the Guardians' Milano

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:00.750
ship was found on set as well.

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:04.190
None of these Guardians connections ended
up in the movie and I wonder if they were

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:07.029
possibly Infinity Stone-related in some way.

00:09:07.029 --> 00:09:11.839
Talking of Infinity Stones, Marvel fans waiting
for the final reveal of the Soul Stone in

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:13.040
Thor: Ragnarok were disappointed.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.940
There were hints and theories that seeing
Hela bathed in an orange light after destroying

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:20.320
Thor's hammer in the trailers indicated she
had acquired the soul stone from Mjolnir,

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:25.790
or after killing Odin, but with that scene
reshot and relocated it's possible that Marvel

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:30.440
midway through production decided to remove
any Soul Stone connection in the movie.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:34.130
There is, however, speculation that the Soul
Stone was in the Eternal Flame which brought

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:38.250
Surtur back to life at the end of the film
and that this could tie in with Thanos's arrival

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:41.569
at the very end in the mid-credits.

00:09:41.569 --> 00:09:45.490
And on the subject of end-credit scenes, Jeff
Goldblum told Digital Spy that, thanks to

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:50.389
Taika Waititi's strong preference for improvisation
on set, they filmed around twenty different

00:09:50.389 --> 00:09:51.889
versions of his post-credits scene.

00:09:51.889 --> 00:09:55.910
Some of them include the Grandmaster trying
to start a new revolution to overthrow the

00:09:55.910 --> 00:10:01.019
current revolution and in another he starts
singing the Sakaaran national anthem.

00:10:01.019 --> 00:10:04.970
Speaking to Fandango, Waititi also said there
was an extended version of the 'Melt Stick'

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:09.450
scene featuring the Grandmaster speaking at
length to his cousin Carlo before he zaps

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.450
him.

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:13.410
And in the scenes at the arena, there were
much lengthier versions of the Grandmaster's

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.829
speeches to the crowd, during which Goldblum
riffed on all kinds of subjects that Waititi

00:10:17.829 --> 00:10:19.920
threw at him including licensing a car!

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.819
Plus, there's a deleted scene involving mime
between the Grandmaster and Topaz, after he

00:10:24.819 --> 00:10:28.060
sends Loki and Valkyrie off to find Thor and
the Hulk.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.800
Waititi's emphasis on improvised comedy in
Thor: Ragnarok means we can probably expect

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:36.629
a lot of unused comedic gems, some of which
will hopefully appear on the Blu-ray and DVD.

00:10:36.629 --> 00:10:41.339
Now, what do you think about any of the deleted
scenes or things which didn't make it into

00:10:41.339 --> 00:10:42.339
Thor Ragnarok?

00:10:42.339 --> 00:10:46.579
Is there anything you wish had been included
or are you happy with the final movie?

